<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>What makes Cylance different?</h4>
<p>Cylance was a pioneer in using AI/ML for malware prevention without signatures. The lightweight agent works offline, making it ideal for air-gapped systems, IoT devices, and environments with limited connectivity.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>Is Cylance now part of BlackBerry?</h4>
<p>Yes. BlackBerry acquired Cylance in 2019. The product is now called BlackBerry Cylance and integrates with BlackBerry's UEM and security portfolio, though it remains a standalone endpoint protection solution.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>Does Cylance work without internet?</h4>
<p>Yes. Cylance's AI model runs locally on the endpoint, providing protection even when offline. This makes it excellent for industrial environments, air-gapped networks, and remote locations with poor connectivity.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>How does Cylance compare to CrowdStrike?</h4>
<p>Cylance excels at prevention and costs less (~$29/yr vs ~$100/yr). CrowdStrike offers superior EDR, threat intelligence, and detection capabilities. Choose Cylance for budget-friendly prevention, CrowdStrike for comprehensive security operations.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>Is Cylance suitable for small businesses?</h4>
<p>Yes. Cylance's affordability (~$29/endpoint/year), simple deployment, and low resource usage make it ideal for small businesses needing effective protection without complexity or high costs.</p>
